## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical schema evolution handling in KCNA beginners: safe deserialization by versioning, backward compatibility, DLQ handling, and testability with canaries.

## Key Concepts
- Schema versioning
- In-memory deserializer registry
- Backward compatibility
- Dead-letter routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example
type Message = { payload: any };
type Deser = (payload: any)=>any;

const registry = new Map<number, Deser>();
registry.set(1, (p)=> ({id: p.id, t: p.time}));
registry.set(2, (p)=> ({id: p.id, ts: p.time, meta: p.meta}));

function deserialize(record: { payload: any }) {
  const v = Number(record.payload?.schema_version ?? 1);
  const deser = registry.get(v) ?? registry.get(1);
  if (!deser) throw new Error('No deserializer');
  return deser(record.payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support field renames without changing consumer code?
- How would you test compatibility across v1 and v2 payloads in a canary run?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of windowed stream processing, stateful aggregations with TTL management, and idempotent output semantics—all critical concepts for reliable event-driven systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Windowing**: Groups time-series events into fixed intervals (tumbling windows) for aggregate computation
- **Per-user state with TTL**: Maintains isolated metrics per user, automatically expiring stale entries to bound memory usage
- **Idempotent outputs**: Ensures deterministic results so duplicate processing (from restarts or retries) produces the same output

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedCTR {
  constructor(windowSizeMs = 5000, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.windowSizeMs = windowSizeMs;
    this.ttlMs = ttlMs;
    this.state = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(msg) {
    const { userId, type, timestamp } = JSON.parse(msg.value);
    const windowStart = Math.floor(timestamp / this.windowSizeMs) * this.windowSizeMs;
    const key = `${userId}:${windowStart}`;

    if (!this.state.has(key)) {
      this.state.set(key, { clicks: 0, impressions: 0, emitted: false, lastUpdate: Date.now() });
    }
    const window = this.state.get(key);
    if (type === 'click') window.clicks++;
    if (type === 'impression') window.impressions++;
    window.lastUpdate = Date.now();

    if (!window.emitted && Date.now() - windowStart >= this.windowSizeMs) {
      this.emitCTR(key, window);
      window.emitted = true;
    }
  }

  commitOffsets(offsets) { /* persist consumer offsets */ }
  windowState() { return Array.from(this.state.entries()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle late arrivals or out-of-order messages?
- How would you test at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design multi-tenant throughput controls with backpressure and strong delivery guarantees in KCNA.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant isolation
- Token bucket rate limiting
- Backpressure signaling to producers
- Idempotent dedupe for at-least-once
